I found the controlling NC Medicaid section and confirmed the document version. Next I’m answering directly from Policy 10A § 3.2.1.6, with the related treatment-services requirements and a federal comparison for context.## Treatment plan / plan of care requirement under NC Medicaid Policy 10A

Under **NC Medicaid Clinical Coverage Policy 10A**, outpatient specialized therapy services must be provided according to a **treatment plan (plan of care)** that includes all of the following:

- the **duration** of the therapy treatment plan, including **start and end dates**, with a term of **no more than six months**;
- the **discipline-specific treatment diagnosis** and any related medical diagnoses;
- the beneficiary’s **rehabilitative or habilitative potential**;
- **defined goals** for each therapeutic discipline that are **specific, measurable, and reasonably expected to be achieved within the duration of the therapy plan**;
- the **skilled interventions, methodology, procedures, and specific programs** to be used;
- the **frequency of services**;
- the **length of each treatment visit in minutes**; and
- the **name, credentials, and signature** of the professional completing the treatment plan, **dated on or before the plan’s start date**.  
  [Clinical Coverage Policy 10A](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/10a-outpatient-specialized-therapies/download?attachment) § 3.2.1.6 (effective Feb. 1, 2025; current version confirmed by amendment check).

## Related implementation requirements

Policy 10A also says:

- services must be provided according to a treatment plan that meets **§ 3.2.1.6**;
- providers must **review and renew or revise treatment plans and goals at least every six calendar months**; and
- a **verbal or written order** must be obtained **before services start**, with detailed rules for signatures, countersignatures, validity period, and no backdating.  
  [Clinical Coverage Policy 10A](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/10a-outpatient-specialized-therapies/download?attachment), “Treatment services must adhere to the following requirements:” (effective Feb. 1, 2025; current version confirmed by amendment check).

## Federal context

The federal outpatient rehabilitation rule similarly requires a **written plan of treatment established before treatment begins**. It must prescribe the **type, amount, frequency, and duration** of therapy services and indicate the **diagnosis and anticipated goals**.  
[42 CFR Part 410](https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-42/part-410) § 410.61 (current version confirmed by amendment check).

## Short answer

So, in plain terms: **NC Medicaid Policy 10A requires a written, discipline-specific plan of care for outpatient therapy, signed and dated on or before the start date, limited to no more than six months, and containing diagnosis, goals, interventions, frequency, visit length, and rehabilitative/habilitative potential. It must be reviewed and renewed or revised at least every six months.**

