I found the key CAP/DA sections and checked amendments. The current CAP/DA policy is the September 1, 2025 version, with no amendments returned by the corpus. Section 3.3 directly governs transfers, and section 5.4.2 confirms beneficiary choice among providers. The federal source in the frame, 42 CFR Part 413, appears to address provider reimbursement rather than beneficiary transfers, so I’m answering primarily from CAP/DA policy and will note that I could not verify a transfer rule there.## Short answer

**Yes.** A CAP/DA beneficiary can transfer between providers, and the CAP/DA policy requires the Case Management Entity (CME) to coordinate the transfer so there are **no gaps in services**. The policy also protects the beneficiary’s **right to choose among providers**.  
Citations: [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment) §§ 3.3, 5.4.2 (effective Sept. 1, 2025; no amendments returned by the corpus).

## Rules governing transfers of eligible beneficiaries

Under **CAP/DA § 3.3, “Transfers of Eligible Beneficiaries,”** the **CME must coordinate the transfer to prevent gaps in service provisions**. Before the transfer, the following must be completed:

- determine the anticipated start date of service;
- coordinate a transition plan between provider agencies;
- discuss and plan for the beneficiary’s health, safety, and well-being;
- initiate transfer of the electronic health record in the CAP Business System (e-CAP/DA) to the receiving county;
- arrange a home visit within **5 business days** after the beneficiary moves to the new primary residence, by the receiving entity, to assess the home environment and identify health and welfare concerns; and
- coordinate services to begin on the **first date of the transfer**.  
Citation: [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment) § 3.3.

## If the beneficiary is transferring to a different county

Section 3.3 gives more specific rules for county-to-county transfers:

- the transferring CME designee must coordinate with the receiving CME designee **on the agreed transfer date or within less than 30 calendar days of the request to transfer**;
- both CMEs must discuss and plan for the beneficiary’s health, safety, and well-being;
- the electronic health record must be transferred to the receiving county;
- the receiving CME must arrange a home visit within **5 business days** of the move; and
- services must begin on the **first date of transfer into the receiving county**.  
Citation: [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment) § 3.3.

## Beneficiary choice of provider

Section **5.4.2** supports the beneficiary’s ability to change or select providers by requiring the person-centered service plan to:

- document the **authorized provider name** for each service; and
- ensure the beneficiary’s **right to choose among providers**, shown by a signed freedom-of-choice form;
- ensure the beneficiary’s right to choose among **Medicaid-enrolled providers**.  
Citation: [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment) § 5.4.2.

## About the federal rule in the frame

I also checked the federal document identified in the frame, **42 CFR Part 413**, and the cited section **§ 413.64** addresses **payments to providers**. I could **not verify from that federal section** any rule governing a CAP/DA beneficiary’s transfer between service providers. So the transfer answer above is grounded in the **NC CAP/DA policy itself**, not that federal reimbursement regulation.  
Citation: [42 CFR Part 413](https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-42/part-413) § 413.64 (current in corpus; no amendments returned).

## Bottom line

A CAP/DA beneficiary **may transfer**, and the governing rules require:

1. **beneficiary choice among Medicaid-enrolled providers**;
2. **CME coordination of the transfer**;
3. **no gap in service delivery**;
4. **electronic record transfer**;
5. **health and safety planning**; and
6. a **home visit within 5 business days** after the move, with services starting on the effective transfer date.  

If you want, I can also pull the surrounding CAP/DA sections to see whether the policy distinguishes between:
- changing only the **direct service provider**, versus
- transferring the beneficiary’s **CME/county assignment**.

I found the current CAP/DA policy document, confirmed it has no separate amendment notice in the corpus, and pulled the key sections the frame pointed to. Next I’m synthesizing the governing coverage rule, the types of home modifications CAP/DA describes, and the specific MDT documentation elements required for a request.## CAP/DA home modifications: requirements and MDT documentation

Under the current **NC Medicaid Clinical Coverage Policy 3K-2, Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA), effective Sept. 1, 2025**, the beneficiary must **work collaboratively with the multidisciplinary team to identify needs for equipment, modification, technology, training and education, and goods and services**. That is the policy’s high-level governing requirement for this category of service. [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)

### 1) What home modifications must be for
CAP/DA equipment/modification/technology must serve one of these purposes:

- improve, maximize, or enhance the beneficiary’s **mobility, safety, or independence**;
- improve **integration into the community**;
- improve the beneficiary’s **environmental and community accessibility**; or
- address the beneficiary’s **24-hour, 7-day-a-week coverage concerns**.

*Cited from* **Policy 3K-2 § 3.4.5**: [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)

### 2) What kinds of home modifications are contemplated
The CAP/DA policy section titled **“Equipment, Modification and Technology”** describes covered home-related modifications in a **primary private residence** where the beneficiary lives, including:

- beneficiary/family-owned residence;
- rented residence **when modifications are portable**; or
- rented residence **when portable modifications are allowed by the owner**.

Examples listed in the policy include:

- ramps;
- grab bars, safety rails, and handrails;
- widening doorways or passages for wheelchair/walker access;
- medically necessary emergency egress;
- bathroom accessibility modifications;
- bedroom modifications for hospital beds and wheelchairs;
- kitchen accessibility modifications;
- floor coverings for ease of ambulation;
- non-skid surfaces;
- door handle or door modifications;
- lifts and related lifting devices;
- environmental control units / electronic aids to daily living;
- replacement of fixtures, mirrors, and storage removed or damaged during an approved modification.

The same section also includes related costs/services such as:

- technical assistance in device selection;
- training in device use by a qualified assistive technology professional;
- purchase costs, permits, inspections, taxes, and delivery charges;
- installation; and
- assessment of the modification by the case manager and applicable inspectors to verify safety and ability to meet the beneficiary’s needs.

*Cited from* **Policy 3K-2, “Equipment, Modificaiton and Technology”**: [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)

### 3) Limits / exclusions relevant to home modifications
The policy also says CAP/DA will not cover items such as:

- items **not of direct medical or remedial benefit**;
- items already covered through **State Plan DME, orthotics, prosthetics, or home health supplies**;
- items that are **recreational in nature**;
- items of **general utility to a non-disabled beneficiary**;
- warranties or unrelated service agreements/maintenance contracts;
- replacements for equipment that was improperly used, lost, or purposely damaged.

*Cited from* **Policy 3K-2, “Limits, Amount and Frequency”**: [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)

## What documentation must the Multidisciplinary Team provide?

For an equipment, technology, or **home modification** request, **§ 5.9.1** requires documentation that includes:

- **a recommendation by the MDT** identifying the beneficiary’s need for the requested home modification;
- that recommendation must **state the cost** of the item/modification;
- **a plan** for how the beneficiary and family will be **trained** in use of the equipment, with completion documented by the case manager and **signed by the CAP/DA beneficiary or responsible party**;
- a **statement of need** identified in the **service plan**;
- **evidence of medical need** recommended by the MDT or by a **physician, when applicable**;
- **shipping costs itemized** in the proposal request;
- any **other information required** for the specific item requested;
- when quotes are required for a purchase, adaptation, or modification, **NC Medicaid determines how many quotes are required**; and
- in special circumstances, **NC Medicaid or its designee determines the appropriate professional(s)** who must make written recommendations.

*Cited from* **Policy 3K-2 § 5.9.1**: [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)

### Additional request/supporting documentation
Other CAP/DA sections also support including:

- a **comprehensive multidisciplinary declaration-of-need assessment** completed by the case manager identifying the equipment, technology, supply, adaptation, or modification need;
- a **copy of the physician’s order**, when applicable;
- a recommendation by an **appropriate professional** identifying the beneficiary’s need;
- the **estimated life of the equipment/modification** and the length of time the beneficiary is expected to benefit; and
- an **invoice from the supplier** showing the date provided and cost, with related charges maintained in the CAP Business system.

*Cited from* **Policy 3K-2, “Limits, Amount and Frequency”**: [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)

## Short practical summary
For **home modifications under CAP/DA**, the request must show that the modification is tied to the beneficiary’s disability-related needs and is intended to improve safety, independence, accessibility, community integration, or round-the-clock coverage needs. The **MDT must document the need**, connect it to the service plan, support medical necessity when applicable, include cost information, and provide any required professional recommendations and training plan documentation.

If you want, I can turn this into a **checklist for a CAP/DA home modification request packet** or a **sample advocacy paragraph** for an appeal or service-plan dispute.

I confirmed the governing document is Clinical Coverage Policy 11A-17, effective April 1, 2025, and checked amendments; the tool shows 11A-17 itself as the current authoritative document. I also pulled the general noncoverage rule and the specific prior-approval documentation section so I can answer with both the coverage criteria and the practical documentation NC Medicaid requires.## NC Medicaid coverage criteria for CAR-T cell therapy

Under **NC Medicaid Clinical Coverage Policy 11A-17, CAR-T Cell Therapy**, CAR-T is covered only if the beneficiary meets the policy’s general coverage rules and the CAR-T-specific criteria. The document returned by the amendments tool is the **current authoritative policy**, effective **April 1, 2025**. [CAR-T Cell Therapy](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/11a-17-car-t-cell-therapy/download?attachment)

### 1. General coverage rules
NC Medicaid requires that the service meet the policy’s general covered-service standards, including that it is:

- individualized, specific, and consistent with the beneficiary’s symptoms or confirmed diagnosis, and not in excess of the beneficiary’s needs;
- safely furnished, with no equally effective and more conservative or less costly treatment available statewide; and
- furnished in a manner not primarily for the convenience of the beneficiary, caretaker, or provider. [CAR-T Cell Therapy](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/11a-17-car-t-cell-therapy/download?attachment)

The policy also says CAR-T is **not covered** if the beneficiary does not meet the eligibility requirements in Section 2.0 or the criteria in Section 3.0, if the service duplicates another provider’s service, or if it is experimental, investigational, or part of a clinical trial. [CAR-T Cell Therapy](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/11a-17-car-t-cell-therapy/download?attachment)

### 2. CAR-T-specific coverage criteria
For CAR-T specifically, NC Medicaid covers the therapy when:

- **the CAR-T therapy has received U.S. FDA approval**;
- **the therapy is administered according to U.S. FDA-approved guidelines** for:
  - indications and usage,
  - dosage and administration,
  - dosage forms and strengths, and
  - warnings and precautions; and
- **the therapy is administered at a certified or authorized treatment center**.

If the particular CAR-T product is subject to an **FDA Risk Evaluation and Mitigation Strategy (REMS)** program, then:

- the **treatment center must be enrolled in the REMS**, and
- providers must be **trained in management of cytokine release syndrome (CRS) and neurologic toxicities**. [CAR-T Cell Therapy](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/11a-17-car-t-cell-therapy/download?attachment)

### 3. Important exclusions / reasons NC Medicaid would deny coverage
The policy’s specific noncovered criteria help clarify the limits. NC Medicaid does **not** cover CAR-T when, among other things:

- the therapy **has not received U.S. FDA approval**;
- it is being administered **outside FDA guidelines** for indications/usage, dosage/administration, or dosage forms/strengths;
- a CAR-T requiring **REMS** is administered at a facility **not enrolled** in that REMS program;
- it is a **repeat treatment** in a beneficiary who previously received another CAR-T therapy;
- the beneficiary’s **psychosocial history** limits ability to comply with pre- and post-infusion care; or
- current beneficiary or caretaker **non-compliance** makes adherence to the required medical regimen improbable. [CAR-T Cell Therapy](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/11a-17-car-t-cell-therapy/download?attachment)

### 4. Prior approval documentation NC Medicaid requires
Although your question was about coverage criteria, the policy also includes specific **prior approval** requirements that often matter in practice. The provider must submit:

- a **letter of medical necessity** signed by the attending physician documenting past chemotherapy regimens and dates, clinical and social history, and indications for CAR-T;
- **verification** that the administering facility is a qualified or authorized treatment center for the requested CAR-T product;
  - if that verification is publicly available, extra documentation is not required;
  - otherwise, the provider must submit a **copy of the contract** between the facility and the manufacturer;
- **serologies** less than 3 months old, including HIV and hepatitis panel;
- diagnostic and procedure results, including **bone marrow biopsy** not more than 6 months old;
- any other diagnostic tests requested; and
- a **complete psychological and social evaluation** addressing:
  - medical compliance,
  - support network,
  - post-treatment care plan with primary and secondary providers identified, and
  - history of mental health issues, substance use, or legal issues. [CAR-T Cell Therapy](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/11a-17-car-t-cell-therapy/download?attachment)

## Bottom line
The core NC Medicaid criteria are:

1. the CAR-T product must be **FDA approved**;
2. it must be used **exactly within FDA-approved parameters**;
3. it must be given at a **certified/authorized treatment center**;
4. if the product has an FDA-required **REMS**, the center must be **REMS-enrolled** and staff must be trained for **CRS and neurologic toxicities**; and
5. the beneficiary must also satisfy NC Medicaid’s general medical-necessity and coverage rules, with documentation supporting clinical appropriateness and ability to comply with care requirements. [CAR-T Cell Therapy](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/11a-17-car-t-cell-therapy/download?attachment)

