I have an initial frame, but it looks partially off-target because it pulled CAP/CAP-DA materials alongside the HIV recertification checklist. I’m going to verify the HIV-specific policy document, read the recertification checklist and the HIV case management policy, and check amendments before answering.I found the HIV-specific policy, Policy 12B, and confirmed via amendment checks that both the HIV case management clinical policy and the recertification checklist appear current in the corpus. Next I’m reading the certification, recertification, decertification, and provider qualification sections in full so I can separate certification requirements from grounds for decertification.I now have the core rules. Policy 12B gives the actual certification and decertification standards, while the DMA-3157 checklist and DMA-3158 application show what recertifying providers must submit and attest to. I’m going to pull the staff qualification sections and any initial-certification context referenced by the application so I can summarize the provider requirements more accurately.## NC Medicaid HIV case management provider certification requirements

Under NC Medicaid, the substantive certification standards for HIV case management providers are in **Clinical Coverage Policy 12B, § 6.2.1**, with the recertification application materials showing what must be submitted to prove ongoing compliance. I checked amendments for both Policy 12B and the recertification checklist/application documents, and the corpus shows them as current with no listed amending change notices. [Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) Case Management](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/12b-human-immunodeficiency-virus-hiv-case-management/download?attachment); [dma-3157 HIV Case Management Provider Recertification Application Checklist](https://policies.ncdhhs.gov/wp-content/uploads/dma-3157.pdf)

### Core certification requirements
A provider must:

- have a **documented successful record of 3 years providing or managing HIV case management programs**;
- ensure HIV case management services are delivered by **qualified case managers** meeting Policy 12B qualifications;
- ensure supervision by **qualified supervisors** meeting Policy 12B qualifications;
- **enroll each physical site** with NC Medicaid as an HIV case management provider;
- comply with **applicable state and federal Medicaid participation laws**;
- **maintain certification** as a qualified HIV case management provider;
- comply with **initial and ongoing certification processes**;
- comply with monitoring and evaluation of case management records through a **quality improvement plan**;
- allow NC Medicaid or its designated contractor to **review beneficiary records and inspect agency operations and financial records**;
- notify NC Medicaid or its designated contractor of proposed business changes within **30 calendar days**, and actual changes within **5 business days**; and
- achieve **national accreditation within 1 year of Medicaid enrollment** from one of the designated accrediting bodies listed in the policy. [Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) Case Management](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/12b-human-immunodeficiency-virus-hiv-case-management/download?attachment), § 6.2.1

### Administrative and operational requirements
The policy also requires the agency to have, among other things:

- a **physical business site** verified by site visit, not in a private residence or vehicle;
- an **organizational chart**;
- disclosure of persons with **5% or more ownership**;
- access to required web-based applications;
- a **business plan** with financial and operational projections/history;
- computer capability for electronic records, HIPAA compliance, data analysis, electronic billing, and EFT;
- a **precertification onsite visit**; and
- compliance with applicable **state and federal licensure and certification requirements**.  
The agency must also maintain written policies on confidentiality, grievances, beneficiary rights, nondiscrimination, ethics, conflicts of interest, electronic records, medical records/retention, freedom of choice, transfer/discharge, and abuse/neglect/exploitation. [Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) Case Management](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/12b-human-immunodeficiency-virus-hiv-case-management/download?attachment), “a. Administrative Requirements”

### Case-management program requirements
The provider must document:

- how it will perform the **core components** of HIV case management;
- staffing/FTEs or functions sufficient to meet those requirements; and
- a **quality improvement plan** addressing health outcomes, record review, complaint tracking, statistical/cost/utilization studies, claims accuracy, and staff qualification compliance. [Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) Case Management](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/12b-human-immunodeficiency-virus-hiv-case-management/download?attachment), “b. Case Management Requirements”

### Staff qualification requirements
Policy 12B also requires qualified case managers and supervisors. For example:

- case managers must meet one of several listed education/experience pathways or certain licensed-professional pathways; [Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) Case Management](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/12b-human-immunodeficiency-virus-hiv-case-management/download?attachment), § 6.1.1
- case managers must be able to assess, plan, link/referral, monitor, and document/attest accurately; id., § 6.1.2
- case managers must have documented HIV knowledge and specified skills/abilities; id., § 6.1.3
- supervisors must meet listed education/experience or licensure pathways and possess supervisory/analytical/program knowledge; id., §§ 6.1.4–6.1.5
- staff must complete required training, including mandatory basic training and annual training. Id., §§ 6.1.7.1–6.1.7.2

## Recertification/documentation requirements

For **recertification**, the provider must:

- submit a **complete and signed Recertification Application** within **60 calendar days of receipt**;
- submit copies of all items from initial certification that have changed;
- submit copies of all HIV case manager and supervisor **credentials**;
- submit annual summaries of **quality improvement activities**, including outcome metrics; and
- submit to an **onsite recertification visit**, including review of records or other clinical/business documentation as needed. [Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) Case Management](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/12b-human-immunodeficiency-virus-hiv-case-management/download?attachment), § 6.2.4

The recertification checklist and instructions add the required submission format and attachments. They show, for example, that recertification is **site-specific** (a separate application for each physical location), and requires:

- title page;
- signed checklist;
- signed application;
- lists of case managers and supervisors with employment dates;
- counties served;
- services offered;
- operating hours and after-hours emergency plan;
- accrediting agency name/dates or explanation;
- satisfaction survey frequency and use of results;
- numbers of Medicaid and Ryan White clients;
- attached policies and plans (confidentiality, grievance, rights, nondiscrimination, ethics, conflict, electronic records, Medicaid records retention, freedom of choice, transfer/discharge, abuse/neglect/exploitation, QA policy/chart, organizational chart, community resources, ownership, staff credentials, CCNC/PCP networking, HR policies/procedures/plans). [dma-3157 HIV Case Management Provider Recertification Application Checklist](https://policies.ncdhhs.gov/wp-content/uploads/dma-3157.pdf); [dma-3158-I HIV Case Management Provider Recertification Application – Instructions](https://policies.ncdhhs.gov/wp-content/uploads/dma-3158-I.pdf); [dma-3158 HIV Case Management Provider Recertification Application](https://policies.ncdhhs.gov/wp-content/uploads/dma-3158.pdf)

The recertification application also requires the provider to agree that Medicaid may review records, that the provider will verify and maintain staff qualifications, provide required supervision, maintain a business plan and computer capability, and comply with applicable law including HIPAA. [dma-3158 HIV Case Management Provider Recertification Application](https://policies.ncdhhs.gov/wp-content/uploads/dma-3158.pdf), Section 4: Compliance

## When can a provider be decertified?

NC Medicaid may decertify an HIV case management provider for any of the grounds listed in **Policy 12B, § 6.2.5**, including:

- **failure to provide core service components**;
- **fraudulent billing practices**;
- owner conviction of a **felony charge**;
- failure to develop, submit, and implement a **written plan of correction** within **30 calendar days** for unmet program requirements cited by NC Medicaid or its designated contractor;
- **falsification of records**;
- violation of a beneficiary’s **confidentiality**;
- employing staff who **do not meet Policy 12B § 6.1 criteria**;
- failure of staff to complete mandatory **basic training within 90 days of employment**;
- failure of staff to obtain required **continuing education units**;
- failure to provide staff with supervision sufficient to meet beneficiaries’ needs;
- failure to submit required documentation within designated time frames;
- failure to maintain documentation sufficient to support billing under **§ 7.5**;
- failure to implement and enforce a **quality improvement program**;
- failure to timely notify NC Medicaid/designated contractor of agency changes, resulting in inability to contact the provider;
- failure to comply with applicable **federal and state laws, regulations, reimbursement plan, and Medicaid policies**;
- failure to **enroll any beneficiaries within 4 months of certification**; and
- failure to achieve or maintain the **certification requirements in § 6.2**. [Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) Case Management](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/12b-human-immunodeficiency-virus-hiv-case-management/download?attachment), § 6.2.5

## Bottom line

In short, an HIV case management provider must be a properly enrolled, site-specific, qualified, supervised, accredited, policy-compliant agency with required written policies, quality improvement systems, staff credentials/training, and recertification submissions. A provider can be decertified for fraud, records issues, confidentiality breaches, unqualified staff, missed training/CEUs, inadequate supervision, missing documentation, failure to correct deficiencies, failure to comply with Medicaid law/policy, failure to serve beneficiaries after certification, or failure to maintain the certification standards themselves. [Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) Case Management](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/12b-human-immunodeficiency-virus-hiv-case-management/download?attachment), §§ 6.2.1, 6.2.4, 6.2.5

